<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aung San Suu Kyi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> Suu Kyi is a Burmese politician, daughter of the independence leader Aung San, who led the democracy movement against military rule from 1988. Her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-league-for-democracy">National League for Democracy</a> won the 1990 election, which the junta ignored, and she spent about fifteen of the following years under house arrest, receiving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1991. She led the government as State Counsellor from 2016 until the coup of 2021, after which she was imprisoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aung-san-suu-kyi.jpg" alt="Aung San Suu Kyi" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cabinet Public Relations Office of the Cabinet Secretariat · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aung_San_Suu_Kyi_at_the_Enthronement_of_Naruhito_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
